{
  "title": "Remember and surface each visitor's recently played games on the cabinet page",
  "open_questions": [
    {
      "id": "what-counts-as-played",
      "question": "Does simply opening a game (landing on its page) count as having played it, or must the person actually start/interact with the game before it is remembered?",
      "required": true
    },
    {
      "id": "clearing-history",
      "question": "Should a person be able to clear or remove entries from their recently played list, or is it fine that it only ever updates as they play?",
      "required": true
    }
  ],
  "criteria": [
    {
      "id": "recent-row-above-grid",
      "title": "A recently played row appears above the full grid, most recent first",
      "guidance": "Open the cabinet page after playing games in a known order. The page shows a labelled section, positioned above the existing full grid of all games, listing the games played most recently first (the newest at the left/top). Each entry launches the same game the grid entry launches. The existing acceptance file acceptance/recently-played.test.js is the check for this; it must go from failing to passing.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "no-duplicates-reorder",
      "title": "Replaying a game moves it to the front instead of adding a second entry",
      "guidance": "Play A, then B, then A again. The recent section lists A, B — two entries, A first — never A, B, A. Assert on the stored order and the rendered order. No existing check can decide this today, so cover it with a unit test alongside tests/cabinet.test.js; do not add a gate binding for it.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "capped-at-three",
      "title": "At most three games are ever shown in the recent section",
      "guidance": "Play four or more distinct games; the recent section renders exactly three entries — the three most recent — and older entries are dropped from both the display and whatever is stored. Cover with a unit test; no existing check can decide this today.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "survives-tab-close",
      "title": "The list survives closing the tab and returning later",
      "guidance": "The history is written to browser-local persistent storage (localStorage) under a single documented key, and is read back on page load. Reloading the page, or closing and reopening the tab, still shows the same recent games in the same order. Decision taken without asking: this is per-browser only — there are no accounts, so the list does not follow a person to another device or browser, and that is acceptable.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "full-grid-unchanged",
      "title": "The full grid still lists every game, exactly as it does now",
      "guidance": "Compare the full grid before and after the change: same games, same order, same markup semantics. Games shown in the recent section still also appear in the full grid below — the shortcut duplicates, it never removes. Cover by extending tests/catalogue.test.js or tests/cabinet.test.js.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "corrupt-state-cannot-break-page",
      "title": "Unreadable or unexpected stored history never breaks the page",
      "guidance": "With the storage key set to each of: absent, empty string, invalid JSON, a JSON object, an array of numbers, an array containing null, and an over-long array — the page still renders the full grid and every game link still works, and no error is thrown to the top level. Decision taken without asking: on any unusable value the code silently treats the history as empty and overwrites it with a clean value on the next play. Storage being unavailable entirely (e.g. throwing on access) is treated the same way. Cover with unit tests.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "retired-games-skipped",
      "title": "History entries for games that no longer exist are skipped silently",
      "guidance": "Put an id in the stored history that is not in the catalogue. The recent section renders only the surviving games (still up to three of the ones that do exist), shows no broken tile and no error text, and the rest of the page is unaffected. Decision taken without asking: unknown ids are dropped quietly rather than shown greyed out or announced.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "empty-state-hidden",
      "title": "A first-time visitor sees the page exactly as it is today",
      "guidance": "With no stored history, the recent section — heading included — is not rendered at all; the page opens straight onto the full grid as it does now. Decision taken without asking: hide rather than show an empty placeholder or a 'nothing yet' message.",
      "required": true,
      "human": false
    },
    {
      "id": "heading-reads-as-mine",
      "title": "The section heading makes it obvious these are the visitor's own games",
      "guidance": "A person reads the heading and copy and judges that it clearly signals 'games you played', not 'games we suggest'. Judgement call on wording and tone.",
      "required": true,
      "human": true
    }
  ],
  "gates": [
    {
      "id": "lint",
      "run": "npm run lint"
    },
    {
      "id": "test",
      "run": "npm test"
    }
  ],
  "gate_bindings": [
    {
      "id": "acceptance-recently-played",
      "run": "node --test acceptance/recently-played.test.js",
      "proves": "recent-row-above-grid"
    }
  ],
  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "recent-history-store",
      "brief": "briefs/recent-history-store.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Build the small module that owns the recently played history: reading it from browser-local persistent storage under one documented key, recording a play, and returning the list of ids to display. Recording a play must move an already-present game to the front rather than appending a duplicate, and the stored list must be trimmed so no more than three ids are ever kept. Every read must be defensive: missing, empty, non-JSON, wrong-shaped, or unavailable storage all resolve to an empty history without throwing, and the next write repairs the stored value. Done looks like a module with unit tests covering ordering, deduplication, the three-item cap, and each malformed-input case, with npm run lint and npm test green."
    },
    {
      "id": "cabinet-recent-section",
      "brief": "briefs/cabinet-recent-section.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Render the recently played section on the cabinet page above the existing full grid, using the history module and the catalogue. It shows up to three games, most recent first, each launching the same target as its grid entry, under a heading that plainly reads as the visitor's own games. Ids in the history that are not in the catalogue are dropped silently, and when the resulting list is empty the whole section — heading included — is not rendered, so a first-time visitor sees today's page unchanged. The full grid must still list every game in its current order, including the games repeated in the recent section. Done looks like acceptance/recently-played.test.js passing, plus tests covering the retired-game and empty cases, with npm run lint and npm test green."
    },
    {
      "id": "record-plays-and-harden",
      "brief": "briefs/record-plays-and-harden.md",
      "dir": ".",
      "objective": "Wire the act of playing a game to the history store so launching a game records it, following whichever definition of 'played' the product answer settles on, and make sure the recording path cannot take the arcade down: any failure in storage or history handling is contained so the page still renders and every game still starts. Done looks like a test that simulates playing several games in sequence and then reloading, showing the expected three-entry, most-recent-first section, plus tests proving the page renders and all game links resolve when the history layer is fed each malformed state, with npm run lint and npm test green."
    }
  ]
}
